    "knowledge_point_id": "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6",
    "knowledge_point": "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13953561/assets/c6b336b55e48a68dcad83efc4a8acd7659225b8f8e29faf8ea176c2cead29901.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?",
    "worked_solution": "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(2,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y-1).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-4,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-4-2)^{2} & =4p(-2-1) \\\\ 36 & =-12p \\\\ p & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =4(-3)(y-1) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
